# 0073-per-game-storage-and-optimize-storage.patch -- one folder per game for its build: the build of the game that is not in use is PARKED in Documents/games/<TITLE ID>/slot (renames, no copy) and moved
#   back when that game needs it; extract/ExeFS is kept, so a rebuild starts from the extraction without the game file; games come back from the builds on disk; Settings > "Optimize storage" replaces a game's
#   files by empty stubs once it is extracted.
# Applies after 0072 (the tree with 0010..0072 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled (dotnet build gameapp.csproj -c Release -r ios-arm64 -t:Compile -nologo: rc 0, 0 errors, 38 warnings: the same set, file
#   and code, as the same project with the 0072 sources) and the pure classes run in a host harness (a net11 console program built from the real source text of GameAppSlot.cs, GameAppUi.cs and the files the
#   0068 / 0071 harnesses compile: 120 checks, all pass; 19 mutations of the new logic, each caught by at least one check; 0068's harness 330 of 330 and 0071's 55 of 55 on this tree, the same as on the 0072
#   tree). No iPhone run. Measured and assumed are told apart below.
#
# WHY (the owner, field facts): storage on the iPhone is the limit. Ocarina of Time: a 25.6 GB .nup, then Documents/extract/RomFS.img 24.4 GB. The owner deleted the .nup in Files and the library showed "0 games"
#   while 24.6 GB of build were on disk. ONE shared slot held the built game, building another game wiped it, and extract/ExeFS (36.6 MB) was deleted after each build (keep_exefs=0), so after an app update
#   (which withdraws the framework: "framework on disk was not built by this version") the rebuild had to read the game file again.
#
# THE SLOT (GameSlot.Items, GameAppSlot.cs). Inventory, from GameAppMain.ConfigText and crates/ibuilder-pipeline/src (layout.rs, markers.rs, chain.rs, profile.rs, assemble.rs, shaders.rs) and ibuilder-nup's
#   extract.rs: extract/ (ExeFS/, RomFS.img, RomFS.img.inputs.json, romfs_programs/, control.nacp, icon.jpg, rewrite/, flat/, shader_out/), DmGameProbe.framework/, profile.tsv, gameapp.framework (the stamp),
#   pipeline.boot, and gameapp.extracted (new, below). Already per game, NOT moved: shaders/<id>, shaders/<id>.work, games/<id>/ (settings, saves, aoc/<n>.img, launcher.json, rewrite-cache, icon, cover),
#   profile-derived/<id>. Not a game's: gameapp.boot-only (a request, written only after the slot is ready), gameapp.args / .continue / .continue.run / .running / .stage / .killed, gameapp.config (rewritten
#   every run), shader_map.tsv (a generic fallback map), .nsz/ (a conversion deleted after use), the logs.
#
# WHAT
#   * keep_exefs=1 in the config (GameAppMain.ConfigText; GAMEAPP_KEEP_EXEFS still overrides: 0 drops it as before). nup= is written only when the .nup has content (NupHasContent): a stub or placeholder is
#     never handed to the chain, which then builds from extract/ (the orchestrator's Rust guard for a zero-byte nup= is a second line of defence, not needed by this patch).
#   * THE EXTRACTION MARKER, Documents/gameapp.extracted (ExtractionMark: game file, base title id, the update / DLC signature, time; written through .part + rename). Deleted in BeginRun when an extraction is
#     about to run (the C# step, or a .nup with content); written by AfterExtraction (GameAppSlotUi.cs) when an extraction is complete: for .nsp .xci .nsz right after the C# step (P.Main rc 0, then its DLC);
#     for a .nup after the chain returned (any outcome) when the chain derived profile.tsv in this run (its mtime >= the run's start: the profile stage runs only once the package is read). The title is checked
#     first: ExeFS/main.npdm's program id (ACI0 + 0x10, as ibuilder-profile reads it), else profile.tsv's, must be the file name's id (when the name has one), and ExeFS/main and a non-empty RomFS.img must be
#     there. ExtractionUse.Usable: an extraction stands in for a game's files when it is whole (ExeFS/main + RomFS.img), the slot's title is the game's, and the marker names this game with the same update / DLC
#     signature (a slot from before this patch: its stamp's game= and content= instead). Not when an update or DLC was added since: the file is asked for, as before.
#   * REBUILD WITHOUT THE FILE: GameAppSourceUi.GameFileHasContent (0068's guard of Set up / Rebuild / Update / "Add to the build queue") is true when the content is missing but ExtractionOnDisk finds a usable
#     extraction (in Documents or parked); SetUpAndPlay then needs no prod.keys; the queue's own start goes on too. BeginRun (every build path: SetUpAndPlay, AutoRunFromArgs, StartContinuedBuild) prepares the
#     slot first and, when the game's files lack content and the extraction in Documents is usable, skips the C# extraction (an .nsp / .xci / .nsz); a .nup is never extracted by C# and gets no nup=. The
#     "framework not built by this version" path is a Set up of a game that is "Needs setup": it goes through the same guards.
#   * PARKING (SlotPlanner / SlotMover / SlotJournal, GameAppSlot.cs; EnsureSlotFor, GameAppSlotUi.cs). Before a build (BeginRun, after the restart-for-run question, before anything writes), before Play
#     (PrimaryTapped's Boot: before AskToRestartForGame writes gameapp.boot-only and before the in-process boot), and before an export (ConfirmExport, before its sizes are measured), Documents is made to hold
#     the game's slot: a slot of another title (by the marker, else profile.tsv, else the stamp's game) is parked in games/<its id>/slot; the game's own parked slot comes back. A slot that names nobody (a read
#     cut short before the chain knew the title) is left where it is, as before (the build overwrites it), and put aside only when the game's own parked slot must come in. One Directory.Move / File.Move per item
#     (rename(2) on the one APFS volume: nothing copied; Directory.Move throws rather than copy across volumes; files never over a file). Something already at the destination (a stale copy) is moved into
#     Documents/.slot-trash/<name>/ first, never overwritten or mixed; those folders are deleted on a worker (Task.Run, not the delete queue: a launch that boots must not wait). Refused while a build or a boot
#     runs, an export runs or is sized, or a delete runs (the log says why and the action does nothing). Main thread: renames and a few stats only, no walk.
#     JOURNAL Documents/gameapp.slot.tsv: `active <TITLE ID>` (the title in Documents, for the log and the person; decisions read the files) and `op park|unpark|trash <arg> <time>`, written BEFORE each op
#     (through .part + rename) and cleared after it. In the session a failing op is rolled back (the renames it made are undone) and the journal cleared. A launch whose last process was killed in the middle
#     (RecoverSlotAtLaunch, in ViewDidLoad right after SweepLeftoverParts: before the library is drawn and before the launch's boot-only / args / continue requests) FINISHES the op (every rename not yet done is done:
#     the moves are idempotent); a recovery that fails keeps the op for the next launch. The journal's argument is a title id or a plain folder name: nothing else is acted on. The launch sweep knows
#     gameapp.slot.tsv.part and gameapp.extracted.part.
#     READY WHILE PARKED: UiFacts.Parked (GatherFacts, from a 5 s cache of the parked slots invalidated by every move) maps the parked games whose stamp this app build made (ClassifyFramework Fresh, framework and
#     flat/ there) to their content=. GameAppUi.GameStatusOf: such a game is Ready (Play, Export IPA, settings, saves, mods, "Rebuild from scratch"); ContentChangedSinceBuild reads its content= ("Update
#     available"); ShowRebuild also when only parked builds are ready. Play brings the slot back first.
#     DELETE: GameAppUi.PlanDeletion adds games/<id>/slot (keep saves; without saves games/<id> goes whole) and gameapp.extracted with the build; the "Frees ..." sizes and the delete are 0064's (worker).
#     CANCEL (GameAppStopUi.ApplyCancel): "free the space" also removes the game's PARKED extraction when it is not a finished build; it never deletes Documents/extract when that holds another title's slot (the
#     paused game's extraction was parked by a Play of another game, whose record still says "extracted"); and a game whose files have no content keeps its extraction (the choice becomes "keep").
#     SIZES: FolderSizes key "parked" (Documents/games/*/slot, a worker walk, rarely: a move or a delete invalidates it) is added to the storage bars' "Builds"; the game sheet's Build line of a parked game
#     measures its games/<id>/slot (key "game-slot").
#   * RECOVERY (SlotRecovery, RecoverGamesFromSlots): at launch and back from the foreground, inside 0071's KeepBuiltGames worker (before its own pass), for the slot in Documents and every parked one that holds a
#     finished build or a RomFS.img and has a title: if no game file of that game is in Documents (its name in any case, or another BASE file of its title id), its placeholder is made again: the built record
#     (BuiltLedger.Seed, the size the import record knew else 0) and 0071's BuiltKeep placeholder (the import ledger first, then a zero-byte file renamed in, never over a file). The name: the stamp's game=, else
#     the marker's, else the built record of its title, else the import record of its title (a base game); only when none knows it, `<title> [<TITLE ID>].<ext>`: the title from extract/control.nacp (the first
#     non-empty of its 16 language entries; "Game" when none; / \ : [ ] * ? " < > | and control characters dropped, no ".." word, no leading dot), the extension .nup when the slot shows a Switch 2 game, else .nsp.
#     The Switch 2 evidence: extract/romfs_programs/ (made only by the NUP reader: ibuilder-nup extract.rs PROGRAMS), or "spec: Ounce" in the rewritten sdk (extract/flat/sdk.patched.bin or
#     extract/rewrite/sdk/sdk.patched.bin: the decompressed image; the same string ibuilder-profile facts.rs reads to give a Switch 2 game its 9 GB pool). Title id ranges were NOT used: nothing in this
#     repository states which ids are Switch 2 games. A Switch game's extraction does not say whether it came from an NSP or an XCI: .nsp is a game file the library lists, and the content is not there either
#     way. The owner's Ocarina (slot in Documents, stamp game= the .nup, file deleted) comes back as its stub by the stamp's name and is Ready without a rebuild (0071 alone already does this from the stamp; the
#     parked slots and the name fallbacks are new).
#   * OPTIMIZE STORAGE (StorageOptimizer, GameAppSlot.cs; the switch in GameAppShelf.cs). It REPLACES 0068's "Remove the game file after a successful build" (one switch; 0068's removal after the build is gone:
#     RemoveSourceAfterBuild now only records the built game, 0071). Key optimize_storage in gameapp.settings.tsv; when absent, 0068's remove_source_after_build is carried over only when the owner had STORED it
#     on; default OFF. When on, right after AfterExtraction wrote the marker: the base file, the update and the DLC that extraction read (a .nup: the .nup) are each replaced by a zero-byte stub when it is a game
#     file (never prod.keys or anything else), of the extracted title (by its file name's id; a file with no id only when it is the base file itself) and has content. The import ledger first (the app's own
#     record, marked removed, its source kept for "Add it again from <source>"; else a new record: how "hand", source "iBuilder's folder"), then 0068's SourceStub.Replace (one rename); a stub that cannot be
#     made puts the ledger back and leaves the file as it was. Not when the person asked to cancel the build and free the space (that deletes the extraction). Any file, not only ones the app imported.
#
# NEW STRINGS: "Optimize storage", "Removes a game's file (.nsp, .xci, .nsz, .nup) once it is extracted. The game stays in the library and plays from its extraction; a later rebuild uses the extraction. Off: the
#   files are kept." Log lines `storage: ...` (the moves, `... (renamed, nothing copied)`, a refusal and why, a recovery, the marker, each stub, a game brought back), `build setting: optimize storage on/off ...`,
#   `build cancelled: ...: its extraction is kept ...` / `... its parked extraction (...) is removed in the background`, `<game>: the game file has no content, its extraction is in Documents/extract: the build
#   starts from it (no extraction)`. ImportHow.ByHand "hand" ("put in iBuilder's folder"). ACCESSIBILITY ID: setting-optimize-storage (replaces setting-remove-source).
#
# MEASURED (host harness, .NET 11, Apple silicon Mac, APFS, scratch folders): marker and journal round trips, garbage, a newline in a field, a journal op naming a path or anything but a title id refused; a slot
#   read (6 items, its title by marker / profile / stamp, npdm program id, a cut npdm); Usable over 9 cases; the planner over 10 cases; EXECUTED on real trees: B built, A built, Play B, Play A: games/<B>/slot
#   holds exactly B's files (SHA-256 of every file), RomFS.img keeps its inode through park and unpark (renamed, not copied), every other file untouched, games/<B>/slot removed and games/<B> kept after the
#   unpark, the journal's active title; a move failing at the third rename: every file back where it was, no op left; a journal that cannot be written: nothing moved; a kill after the journal and after 0, 1 or
#   2 renames of a park, an unpark, a trash: Recover finishes it, a second Recover does nothing, a failing recovery keeps the op; a stale copy at the destination goes to .slot-trash; recovery names (stamp,
#   marker, built record, import record, synthesized), NACP title, Switch 2 evidence, Decide over 5 cases, a parked build whose file is gone comes back as a stub with its built record; the owner's layout
#   (stamp game= the .nup, no ExeFS): stub, Ready and Play, a rebuild not offered from that extraction until ExeFS is there; the toggle (default, own key, carried over, precedence); the optimizer's plan (base,
#   update, DLC by id; another title, a no-id non-base file, prod.keys, a .txt, missing, empty, an unknown extracted title) and its run (two stubs with the right records, another game's file and prod.keys keep
#   their SHA-256, run again does nothing, a failing rename leaves the file and the ledger as they were); UiFacts.Parked: Ready / Play / Needs setup, Rebuild offered, Update available, PlanDeletion.
#   Mutations caught (19): BelongsTo without the title, no put-aside, no roll back, Usable without the content or the title, the optimizer without the title check, the toggle on by default, Recover not
#   finishing, Decide missing another file of the title, any trash name accepted, trashing a nobody slot always, parking the game's own slot, no journal before a move, parked games not Ready, Delete keeping
#   the parked slot, a new stub record not removed, no ledger roll back, no Switch 2 evidence from the sdk, brackets kept in a synthesized name.
# NOT EXERCISED / ASSUMED (no device run): everything UIKit and every hook in GameAppViewController (EnsureSlotFor before Play / a build / an export, the launch recovery, AfterExtraction's two call sites, the
#   switch, the sheet's Build line, the storage bars, the cancel guards); that Directory.Move of a 24 GB extract/ on the phone is a rename (APFS, one volume: expected, not measured there); that the chain
#   builds a game from extract/ with no nup= and the C# step skipped (by reading chain.rs: exe_dir defaults to extract/ExeFS, the profile derives from extract/); that profile.tsv's mtime is a reliable sign the
#   NUP read finished on the phone; that keep_exefs=1 is honoured by drop_exefs (read in profile.rs, not run); a kill in the middle of a real park on the phone; the recovery racing a move the person starts in
#   the first second of a launch (both read the slot; the recovery only adds placeholders for games with no file).
# LIMITS / RISKS: every game now keeps its ExeFS (36.6 MB for Ocarina, 80 MB for Grizzy). The owner's existing Ocarina extraction has no ExeFS: its first rebuild after an app update still needs the file once.
#   0066's "Delete the build?" after a full export still deletes extract/ (its text says the game file stays: not true for a game "Optimize storage" emptied; the game then needs its file again to be rebuilt).
#   0065's cleanup and 0066's cleanup do not delete gameapp.extracted (their exact lists are checked by their harnesses): a marker left alone is harmless (a usable extraction needs ExeFS/main and RomFS.img).
#   A game whose file name has no title id and whose id was never read cannot name games/<id>/slot when deleted: its parked slot stays. A slot that names nobody stays in Documents until a parked slot must come
#   back (then it is put aside and deleted). A paused build whose extraction a later build replaced (0065's ExtractReplacedBy) resumes "from the start", but its parked extraction comes back first and, when the
#   file is a stub with a marker, the extraction is not run again. The parked list is cached 5 s: a change made outside the app shows up to 5 s late.
